'''apanthropy''' (a-pan′thrọ̄-pi), ''n.'' [< Gr. ἀπανθρωπία, < ἀπάνθρωπος, unsocial, < ἀπό, from, + ἄνθρωπος, man: see ''anthropic''.] An aversion to

readings.c1s2:
the company of men; a love of solitude; in ''morbid psychol.'', a species of melancholy marked by a dislike of society.

'''apar, apara''' (ap′ä̤r, ap′a̤-rä̤), ''n.'' [S. Amer.] The mataco; the tolypeutine or three-banded arma-
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|Apar, or three-banded Armadillo (''Tolypeutes tricinctus'').}}}}
dillo of South America (''Dasypus'' or ''Tolypeutes tricinctus''), a small species capable of rolling itself up into a complete ball. {{smaller|It is also notable for walking on the tips of the fore claws, the two outer toes being much reduced, while the third is greatly developed. There are other species of ''Tolypeutes'' (which see).}}

'''aparejo''' (ä-pä-rā′hō), ''n.'' [Sp., a pack-saddle; a particular use of ''aparejo'', preparation, harness, gear, tackle, pl. ''aparejos'', apparatus: see ''apparel''.] A kind of Mexican saddle formed of leather cushions stuffed with hay, used in the western United States.

